// `doc/plan-blas-native.md` §7.1 — BLAS callable explicitly from cleave, as
// an ordinary scientific-library primitive. One generic, low-level binding
// (`raw_sgemm`), not the first attempt's own four shape-specific wrappers
// (`cleave_blas_sgemm_rowmajor`/`transpose_a`/`transpose_b`/`bias_rowmajor`,
// each hardcoding its own `CBLAS_TRANSPOSE` flags) — `cleave-rt`'s own
// `cleave_blas_sgemm` (`cleave-rt/src/lib.rs`) already exposes `cblas_sgemm`
// generically, real runtime `trans_a`/`trans_b` flags included; this module
// is the thin cleave-level surface over it, nothing more.
//
// Algebra-based, like every other stdlib primitive here (`MatMul`, not a
// bare top-level generic `fn`) — deliberately, not a style choice: an
// `extern fn` can never itself be generic ("only concrete, monomorphized
// signatures can cross a C-ABI boundary", found by direct testing), so each
// concrete `(A0,A1,B0,B1,C0,C1)` instantiation needs its *own* monomorphized
// extern declaration — exactly what one `impl<const...>` per instantiation
// already gives for free, the same mechanism `matmul`'s own `N`/`M`/`K` are
// inferred through (never a turbofish), unlike a top-level generic `fn`
// (tried first, failed: its own const generics didn't infer from the
// argument's concrete `Tensor` shape the way an algebra impl's do).
//
// **Deliberately not `#[pure]`.** `raw_sgemm`'s own real effect — writing
// into `c`'s own underlying buffer — is entirely invisible to cleave's own
// dataflow (its declared return type is `()`; the write happens through a
// raw pointer the egraph never looks inside). `#[pure]` would tell `egraph
// .rs::is_pure_prim_op` this call is safe to fold/reorder/treat as one
// opaque, freely-reorderable node (`Forward::walk`'s own doc comment) —
// exactly the same unsoundness `Print<T>::print`'s own order-dependent
// effect already establishes this flag must never claim for a call whose
// real effect isn't captured in its own return value. Ordinary (non-pure)
// externs are never eliminated even when their result is unused (`doc/
// backlog.md`'s own "an extern call is never eliminated even when unused"
// finding) — exactly the protection this call's own side effect needs.
//
// **Explicit destination, no scratch buffer, nothing for `dps_rewrite.rs`
// to redirect after the fact** (`doc/plan-affine-ownership.md` §15's own
// real conclusion this session: neither `--linalg-fuse-elementwise-ops`
// nor One-Shot Bufferize's empty-tensor-elimination can ever reach an
// `extern` call — the destination has to be decided by cleave's own
// emitted code, directly, which is exactly what `c` being a real, explicit
// parameter here means).
algebra RawSgemm<A, B, C> {
    fn raw_sgemm(
        trans_a: i32,
        trans_b: i32,
        m: i32,
        n: i32,
        k: i32,
        alpha: f32,
        a: A,
        lda: i32,
        b: B,
        ldb: i32,
        beta: f32,
        c: C,
        ldc: i32
    );
}

// `[f32;A0,A1]` (a plain array), *not* `Tensor<f32,A0,A1>` — deliberate,
// not a simplification: only `Ty::Array` crosses an extern boundary as the
// `(pointer, length)` pair `cleave_blas_sgemm`'s own Rust signature expects
// (`mlir_lower.rs::lower_prim_op`'s own `PrimOp::Extern` arm, `matches!(t,
// Ty::Array(..))` — checked directly, not assumed after this exact
// mismatch produced silently-wrong values and a real OpenBLAS "illegal
// value" rejection). A `Tensor` crosses differently (its own bufferized
// form) — `Sgemm::sgemm` below converts explicitly via `mlir::
// bufferization::to_buffer` first, mirroring the first attempt's own
// identical, already-proven `let a_buf: [T;P,Q] = mlir::bufferization::
// to_buffer(a);` pattern (`stdlib/linalg/matrix.cleave`'s own former
// `MatMulTransposeB` impl).
impl<const A0: i32, const A1: i32, const B0: i32, const B1: i32, const C0: i32, const C1: i32>
    RawSgemm<[f32;A0,A1], [f32;B0,B1], [f32;C0,C1]>
{
    extern(cleave_blas_sgemm) fn raw_sgemm(trans_a, trans_b, m, n, k, alpha, a, lda, b, ldb, beta, c, ldc);
}

// The real, ergonomic entry point — `sgemm(trans_a, trans_b, alpha, a, b,
// beta, c)`, `doc/plan-blas-native.md` §7.1's own literal signature. `m`/
// `n`/`k`/`lda`/`ldb`/`ldc` derived from `a`/`b`/`c`'s own declared shapes,
// never asked of the caller directly: row-major leading dimension is
// always an operand's own *physical* trailing extent, regardless of
// `trans_a`/`trans_b` (real BLAS reads a transposed operand's memory as-is,
// no materialized copy, matching the native `matmul_transpose_a/b`'s own
// `indexing_maps` trick exactly). Deliberately unchecked which of `A0`/
// `A1` is really "rows" vs "cols" beyond what `trans_a`/`trans_b` select —
// six independent const generics, no compile-time relationship enforced
// between them — the same low-level, unchecked contract the real
// `cblas_sgemm` C ABI itself has (§7.4: "l'API BLAS explicite est bas
// niveau et non différentiable au départ"); correctness is the caller's
// own responsibility, exactly as for any other direct BLAS call.
//
// Returns a *new* tensor value built from the exact buffer `raw_sgemm`
// wrote into — never the caller's original `c` binding unchanged. Found
// the hard way, not assumed: `c` (a `Tensor`) and `c_buf` (the `[f32;C0,
// C1]` `mlir::bufferization::to_buffer(c)` produces) are not provably the
// same underlying memory to cleave's own dataflow (a `Tensor` is value-
// semantics; bufferization decides where its real backing memref lives,
// same reason `to_buffer` exists at all instead of a bare cast) — the
// first version of this function returned `c` itself and got back exactly
// its own pre-write zeros every time, matching the old wrapper's own
// established shape exactly (`stdlib/linalg/matrix.cleave`'s former
// `matmul_transpose_b`: `let mut c_buf: [T;P,R] = mlir::memref::alloc();
// blas_sgemm_transpose_b(...); mlir::bufferization::to_tensor(c_buf,
// restrict: "unit")` — that code never returned an input tensor either,
// it built a *fresh* buffer and returned `to_tensor` of *that*). The one
// real difference here: `c` starts from the *caller's own* `to_buffer(c)`
// rather than a fresh `memref::alloc()`, because `beta != 0.0` needs
// `raw_sgemm` to read `c`'s own prior values before overwriting them —
// `alpha*a@b + beta*c`, not just `alpha*a@b`.
algebra Sgemm<A, B, C> {
    fn sgemm(trans_a: bool, trans_b: bool, alpha: f32, a: A, b: B, beta: f32, c: C) -> C;
}

impl<const A0: i32, const A1: i32, const B0: i32, const B1: i32, const C0: i32, const C1: i32>
    Sgemm<Tensor<f32,A0,A1>, Tensor<f32,B0,B1>, Tensor<f32,C0,C1>>
{
    fn sgemm(trans_a, trans_b, alpha, a, b, beta, c) {
        let m: i32 = if trans_a { A1 } else { A0 };
        let k: i32 = if trans_a { A0 } else { A1 };
        let n: i32 = if trans_b { B0 } else { B1 };
        let ta: i32 = if trans_a { 1 } else { 0 };
        let tb: i32 = if trans_b { 1 } else { 0 };
        let a_buf: [f32;A0,A1] = mlir::bufferization::to_buffer(a);
        let b_buf: [f32;B0,B1] = mlir::bufferization::to_buffer(b);
        let mut c_buf: [f32;C0,C1] = mlir::bufferization::to_buffer(c);
        raw_sgemm(ta, tb, m, n, k, alpha, a_buf, A1, b_buf, B1, beta, c_buf, C1);
        mlir::bufferization::to_tensor(c_buf, restrict: "unit")
    }
}
